.include "8clocks.jsim"
.include "nominal.jsim"
.include "stdcell.jsim"

* We first used De Morgan's law, AB + CDE + FG becomes all NANDs
* NOT(NOT(AB) NOT(CDE) NOT(FG)).
* The first three NANDs give NOT(AB), NOT(CDE), and NOT(FG).
* The last NAND combines the three NANDs so that it simplifies back out to normal so this has 4 gates and 2 levels
* Basically we have 2 nand2 and 2 nand3 in the composition after DeMorgans
* nand2 and nand3 we also found in both quickref.pdf and stdcell.jsim
.subckt fx a b c d e f g out
Xab a b n_ab nand2
Xcde c d e n_cde nand3
Xfg f g n_fg nand2
Xout n_ab n_cde n_fg out nand3
.ends

* First we did, p = a XOR b, the we will XOR p with the carry_in because full adder + half adder
* For the carry output, a carry happens when at least two of the three inputs are 1.
* This can be written as: carry = AB + p*carry_in
* formula. Reusing p saves gates, and three NANDs handle the carry.
* Since p = A XOR B, this gives the correct full-adder carry behavior.
* 
* XOR gates are used for the sum because they implement the required sum operation
* with fewer gates, compared to rebuilding the XR using only NAND/NOR/NOT gates
* NAND gates are then used for the carry because they can implement the required
* AND/OR behavior efficiently using De Morgan's Law
.subckt fa a b carry_in sum carry
Xp a b p xor2
Xsum p carry_in sum xor2
Xab a b n_ab nand2
Xpc p carry_in n_pc nand2
Xcarry n_ab n_pc carry nand2
.ends

Xfx clk1 clk2 clk3 clk4 clk5 clk6 clk7 OUT fx
Xfa clk1 clk2 clk3 sum carry fa

* quickref.pdf states that a clock period is 2*tstable + tAtoB + tBtoA.
* For clk7, this is 2*319.9ns + 0.1ns + 0.1ns = 640ns (One full cycle)
.tran 640ns
.plot clk1
.plot clk2
.plot clk3
.plot clk4
.plot clk5
.plot clk6
.plot clk7
.plot OUT
.plot sum
.plot carry
